statement:
Every string in `words` is made of lowercase English letters and never repeats a letter, so think of each string as its *set* of letters. Two strings are **connected** when one string's letter set can become the other's through exactly one of these moves:

- add a single letter;
- remove a single letter;
- swap a single letter for some letter (the swap may put back the same letter, so two strings with identical letter sets are connected).

Split `words` into groups so that connected strings always land in the same group, using as many groups as possible. In other words, each group is one connected component of the graph whose edges are the connections; a string with no connections is a group by itself. This split is always unique.

Return an array of two integers: the number of groups, and the number of strings in the largest group.

validator:
function validate([words]) {
  if (!Array.isArray(words) || words.length < 1 || words.length > 20000) return 'words needs 1 to 2 * 10^4 strings.';
  for (const word of words) {
    if (typeof word !== 'string' || word.length < 1 || word.length > 26) return 'Each word has 1 to 26 letters.';
    if (!/^[a-z]+$/.test(word)) return 'Words use lowercase English letters only.';
    if (new Set(word).size !== word.length) return 'A word may not repeat a letter.';
  }
  return null;
}

